                <article>

                    <h1>C# (C-Sharp)</h1>

                    <p><strong>C#</strong> was created by Microsoft and released in July 2000, designed by Anders
                        Hejlsberg as
                        the flagship language of the new .NET Framework. It was built largely as Microsoft's answer to
                        Java,
                        which was rapidly winning over enterprise developers with its cross-platform promise. Microsoft
                        needed a
                        modern, type-safe, object-oriented language of its own — one that combined the power of C++ with
                        the
                        productivity of Java, while integrating tightly with Windows.
                    </p>

                    <h2>From Windows-Only to Cross-Platform</h2>
                    <p>For its first decade, C# ran exclusively on Windows through the .NET Framework. That changed in
                        <strong>2016</strong> when Microsoft released <strong>.NET Core</strong>, a complete rewrite of
                        .NET
                        that was open-source, modular, and able
                        to run on Windows, Linux, and macOS. For the first time, C# code could be compiled and executed
                        natively
                        on Linux servers and
                        containers.
                    </p>

                    <p>In <strong>2020</strong>, Microsoft unified everything under a single platform simply called .NET
                        (starting with .NET 5), dropping the "Core" branding. Today, C# is a fully cross-platform
                        language used
                        to build web apps, cloud
                        services, mobile apps, and games on virtually any operating system.
                    </p>


                    <h2>Key Features</h2>
                    <ul class="features">
                        <li>Object-oriented and type-safe</li>
                        <li>Automatic memory management</li>
                        <li>Modern features like LINQ, async/await, and generics</li>
                        <li>Cross-platform via the unified .NET runtime</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2>Common Uses</h2>
                    <ul class="features">
                        <li>Web development (ASP.NET Core)</li>
                        <li>Cloud-based applications (Azure)</li>
                        <li>Game development (Unity)</li>
                        <li>Cross-platform desktop and mobile apps (.NET MAUI)</li>
                    </ul>
                </article>

